WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.549 --> 00:00:04.590
In the fourth Transformers movie, there's
a scene where a random guy in an elevator

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.660
helps Stanley Tucci beat someone up.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:14.599
That guy? Turns out he's a Chinese boxer,
Zou Shiming - a world champion &amp; gold medalist.

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:21.019
Elsewhere in the movie, you’ll see a Chinese
milk box, and even a Chinese bank ATM in Texas

00:00:21.019 --> 00:00:22.930
of all places.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:30.649
If you didn't recognize these references,
that's because they weren't meant for you.

00:00:30.649 --> 00:00:35.000
The growth of China’s middle class has created
a massive new market for the entertainment

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.000
industry.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.170
Next year China’s box office revenue will
likely surpass the US, making it the largest

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:43.910
movie market in the world.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:48.879
China has built 27 new cinema screens per
day on average this year, and as of November

00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:54.120
2016, the country has more screens in total
than the 40,475 in the US.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:58.329
Obviously, the movie makers in Hollywood want
to reach those customers. Transformers 4,

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:03.480
a movie criticized for making literally no
sense at all, was the only film in 2014 to

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.100
collect over 1 billion dollars worldwide at
the box office, thanks to Chinese viewers.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:13.560
The problem is the Chinese government only
allows a certain number of foreign films into

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.820
the country each year.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:19.020
And each one has to pass through the government’s
censorship agency.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.100
There's kind of contradictory impulses. On
the one hand, China wants to be the best at

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:30.710
everything. They want to succeed. On the other
hand, they want to promote what the leader

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:34.360
is promoting. Chinese propaganda and socialist
core values.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:40.140
Before the 1990s, very few Hollywood movies
made it to Chinese audiences.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:46.500
The Chinese government had its own film industry
to distribute propaganda, but it was failing.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:54.820
In 1979 $23.9 billion tickets were purchased
in 1993 that dropped to $9.5 billion.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:02:00.000
In 1994 things started to change. The Fugitive
became the first new American film set for

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.140
a general release to the Chinese public.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:07.730
It was so popular that scalpers outside theaters
were getting double the price of the ticket.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:13.770
One dollar and twenty-five cents.
Ten foreign movies were allowed in 1994. Since

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:19.160
then, Hollywood has pushed the U.S. government
to continually negotiate for higher quotas.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:25.550
These days, a U.S. film typically makes it
into a Chinese movie theater in one of three

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:26.870
ways.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:32.000
Through revenue-sharing, co-producing with
a Chinese company, or through a flat fee.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.280
The most common is the revenue-sharing model
where the studio gets 25% of the revenue.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.400
But only 34 foreign films per year are allowed.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.070
Over the last 10 years American films have
strategically incorporated positive Chinese

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:50.490
story elements to bolster their chances of
being one of the films selected.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:56.340
In Red Dawn, the enemy was originally China
but was changed to North Korea in post production.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:04.780
In the film 2012 Oliver Platt says

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:07.410
praising China for building arks in advance.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:12.290
World War Z the book had the virus start in
china due to illegal organ trade that’s

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:14.020
not the case in the movie.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:19.530
In the Martian, the Chinese space industry
saves the day. The Chinese based Bona Film

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:23.550
Group invested millions in the film.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:30.230
It’s important to note though that studios
don’t have to do this. Harry Potter is great

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:31.230
example.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:35.840
If you look at the regulations in a very strict
sense, theoretically something like a Harry

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.630
Potter film should not be shown because you're
not supposed to have superstition and wizards

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:47.380
and things like that. But it's very hard to
deny the Chinese audience Harry Potter.

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:50.349
There are two ways to get around the 34-film
limit.

00:03:50.349 --> 00:03:55.180
The least popular among big Hollywood studios
today is the flat-fee model because they’re

00:03:55.180 --> 00:04:00.180
selling the film at fraction of the cost and
China gets 100% of the ticket sales.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:05.580
The other option is co-producing the movie
with a Chinese company so that it’s not

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:11.620
technically a foreign film. But co-productions
are the most tightly regulated, with strict

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:16.320
guidelines on things like the film’s shooting
location and its finances. It has to also

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.879
have at least ⅓ Chinese actors in the cast.
In short, China somehow has to play a significant

00:04:21.879 --> 00:04:24.710
role in the film, and it can’t be as the
villain!

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:27.039
Drop your weapons! Or I kill the man!

00:04:27.039 --> 00:04:32.110
Before Looper was released its director and
studio partnered with DMG, a Chinese based

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:36.229
entertainment company to help adapt the film
to a Chinese audience. DMG invested 40% in

00:04:36.229 --> 00:04:41.379
the film too. The script was re-written to
take place in Shanghai rather than its original

00:04:41.379 --> 00:04:43.370
location, Paris.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:48.029
But ultimately, separate American and Chinese
versions of Looper were released because the

00:04:48.029 --> 00:04:52.169
Chinese scenes in the film didn’t resonate
with U.S. and other international markets

00:04:52.169 --> 00:04:53.169
audiences.

00:04:53.169 --> 00:04:57.680
That’s always the issue when you’re dealing
with China and deciding on a co-production.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.919
As important as the China market is, it’s
not the only market.

00:05:00.919 --> 00:05:06.620
Ultimately China wants their own films to
outnumber and outplay their foreign competitors

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:08.650
so they’re building their own Hollywood.

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:14.830
It’s an $8.2 billion dollar investment slated
to open it’s doors in April 2017 from the

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:21.460
same company that bought AMC in 2012 and subsequently
doubled their ticket sales.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:26.139
Sure, China will share their facilities with
U.S. studios but their doors are still only

00:05:26.139 --> 00:05:31.930
half open. That film quota that has held the
US at bay for the last two decades will also

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:57.159
apply to Hollywood studios vying to book the
state of the art facilities.

